// REJECTS: Unexpected token 'UserList'. Valid alternatives: ['Deployment']
//
// `ownership: UserList { users: [...] }` on a FunctionJar. HostedService accepts exactly two
// ownership arms -- `UserList { users: [...] }` and `Deployment { identifier: '...' }`, both
// exercised in ../fixtures/t2-hostedservice.pure -- and FunctionJar accepts only the second:
//     functionJarOwnership : FUNCTIONJAR_OWNERSHIP COLON deployment SEMI_COLON
// with no alternation at all.
//
// The two grammars share the `ownership` keyword, the `Deployment` keyword, the `identifier`
// keyword and the surrounding element shape, and they are usually described together as "the
// function activators". A rewrite with one shared ownership parser is more permissive here
// and nothing downstream would notice: DeploymentOwner and UserListOwner are both valid
// protocol classes, so the extra arm produces a well-formed model of an element kind that
// cannot exist.
//
// Same story, opposite direction, for the REQUIRED-ness of the field: see
// ./neg-memsqlfunction-missing-ownership.pure -- MemSqlFunction requires `ownership` and
// BigQueryFunction, whose grammar is otherwise identical, does not.
###FunctionJar
FunctionJar fixture::neg::UserOwnedJar
{
   ownership: UserList { users: ['user1'] };
   function: fixture::neg::f():String[1];
}
